{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#def:pet-ch2-dual-one-form","title":"def:pet-ch2-dual-one-form","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives.md","line":261,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives.tex#def:pet-ch2-dual-one-form","uses":[],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives.tex","label":"def:pet-ch2-dual-one-form","line":553,"source":"\\begin{definition}[dual $1$-form $\\theta_X$]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch2-dual-one-form}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0069}\n\n  For a vector field $X$ on $(M,g)$, the dual $1$-form is\n  $\\theta_X:=i_Xg$, i.e.\\ $\\theta_X(Y)=g(X,Y)$. $X$ is locally a gradient field\n  iff $d\\theta_X=0$; in general $d\\theta_X$ measures the extent to which $X$\n  fails to be a gradient field.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[dual <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$1$\">$1$</span>-form <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\theta _X$\">$\\theta _X$</span>] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368322d6475616c2d6f6e652d666f726d\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source\" style=\"display:block\">Source: <span class=\"reference\">petersen:page-0069</span></span> <p></p>For a vector field <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$X$\">$X$</span> on <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(M,g)$\">$(M,g)$</span>, the dual <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$1$\">$1$</span>-form is <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\theta _X:=i_Xg$\">$\\theta _X:=i_Xg$</span>, i.e. <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\theta _X(Y)=g(X,Y)$\">$\\theta _X(Y)=g(X,Y)$</span>. <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$X$\">$X$</span> is locally a gradient field iff <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$d\\theta _X=0$\">$d\\theta _X=0$</span>; in general <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$d\\theta _X$\">$d\\theta _X$</span> measures the extent to which <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$X$\">$X$</span> fails to be a gradient field. ","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#def:pet-ch2-covariant-derivative-euclidean","next":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#def:pet-ch2-killing-field","relations":[],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#prop:pet-ch2-covariant-derivative-implicit-euclidean","title":"prop:pet-ch2-covariant-derivative-implicit-euclidean","label":"prop:pet-ch2-covariant-derivative-implicit-euclidean"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#rem:pet-ch2-koszul-formula","title":"rem:pet-ch2-koszul-formula","label":"rem:pet-ch2-koszul-formula"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#prop:pet-ch2-divergence-adjoint-formula","title":"prop:pet-ch2-divergence-adjoint-formula","label":"prop:pet-ch2-divergence-adjoint-formula"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#rem:pet-ch2-ex-7","title":"rem:pet-ch2-ex-7","label":"rem:pet-ch2-ex-7"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#rem:pet-ch2-ex-29","title":"rem:pet-ch2-ex-29","label":"rem:pet-ch2-ex-29"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives#def:pet-ch2-dual-one-form","title":"def:pet-ch2-dual-one-form","tex":"\\begin{definition}[dual $1$-form $\\theta_X$]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch2-dual-one-form}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0069}\n\n  For a vector field $X$ on $(M,g)$, the dual $1$-form is\n  $\\theta_X:=i_Xg$, i.e.\\ $\\theta_X(Y)=g(X,Y)$. $X$ is locally a gradient field\n  iff $d\\theta_X=0$; in general $d\\theta_X$ measures the extent to which $X$\n  fails to be a gradient field.\n\\end{definition}","source":"tex/references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch02_Derivatives.tex","description":"","dependencies":[],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}